          "rules": [
            {
              "id": "CWE-22",
              "name": "Path traversal / arbitrary file read in download() via unvalidated filename",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Path traversal / arbitrary file read in download() via unvalidated filename"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The **`download()`** function in `app.py` joins a user-supplied `filename` onto the administrator-configured `root` without any validation or containment check, allowing arbitrary file reads anywhere the process can access. Both relative traversal sequences (`../../`) and absolute paths (which entirely replace the root when joined) escape the intended download root."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The **`download()`** function in `app.py` joins a user-supplied `filename` onto the administrator-configured `root` without any validation or containment check, allowing arbitrary file reads anywhere the process can access. Both relative traversal sequences (`../../`) and absolute paths (which entirely replace the root when joined) escape the intended download root.\n\nAn attacker able to supply the `filename` parameter can read any file accessible to the application process, including files outside the download root: credentials, configuration, source code, and other users' data. This fully breaks the documented trust boundary that restricts downloads to the configured root.\n\nReplace `download()` with the containment logic already implemented in `download_v2()`: resolve the root once, resolve the joined target, and verify the resolved target is relative to the resolved root (e.g. `Path.is_relative_to`) before opening the file, rejecting otherwise. Alternatively, restrict filenames to a safe character set and reject separators and absolute paths, though the resolve-and-containment approach is the robust fix because it holds after symlink resolution.",
                "markdown": "The **`download()`** function in `app.py` joins a user-supplied `filename` onto the administrator-configured `root` without any validation or containment check, allowing arbitrary file reads anywhere the process can access. Both relative traversal sequences (`../../`) and absolute paths (which entirely replace the root when joined) escape the intended download root.\n\nAn attacker able to supply the `filename` parameter can read any file accessible to the application process, including files outside the download root: credentials, configuration, source code, and other users' data. This fully breaks the documented trust boundary that restricts downloads to the configured root.\n\nReplace `download()` with the containment logic already implemented in `download_v2()`: resolve the root once, resolve the joined target, and verify the resolved target is relative to the resolved root (e.g. `Path.is_relative_to`) before opening the file, rejecting otherwise. Alternatively, restrict filenames to a safe character set and reject separators and absolute paths, though the resolve-and-containment approach is the robust fix because it holds after symlink resolution."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "6.5",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-22",
                  "stride:T",
                  "stride:I"
                ]
              },
